{
  "description": ["Component-level benchmarks are the 'unit' tests of the hand, focusing on the performance of one or a few components.",
                  " At this level, system designers have the most control of performance, but the least insight into how their decisions affect dexterity."],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Actuators",
      "description": "Actuators can act about an axis (torque) or along a line (force). To address this in the benchmarks, forces and torques are generally referred to as efforts. When reporting performance scores, whether the actuator produces torques or forces needs to be defined.",
      "benchmarks": [
        {
          "id": "output-inertia",
          "title": "Output Inertia",
          "shortDescription": "",
          "description": "Actuator output inertia is a dynamic property of the actuator that affects control bandwidth and force transparency. An actuator with larger inertia experiences a reduction in control bandwidth. Adding a speed reduction to the actuator, such as a gearhead for motors, amplifies the actuator inertia as felt by the environment (reflected inertia). The reflected inertia scales with N\u00B2, where N is the transmission ratio of the speed reduction. Reflected inertia amplification combined with transmission friction has significantly negative effects on a finger's mechanical transparency. Along with negatively affecting transparency and force control bandwidth, there is a tradeoff between actuator inertia and strength. Decreasing actuator inertia improves mechanical transparency and bandwidth, but it also reduces strength.",
          "procedures": [""],
          "metrics": [{ "name": "", "unit": "", "description": "" }],
          "analysisMethods": [""]
        },
        {
